<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmadou Ahidjo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmadou Ahidjo was a Cameroonian politician from the Muslim north who became prime minister of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> in 1958 and led it to independence in 1960. As first president of Cameroon he united it with the former British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> in 1961, created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and ruled until he resigned in 1982 in favour of Paul Biya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadou-ahidjo.jpg" alt="Ahmadou Ahidjo" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Series: Reagan White House Photographs, 1/20/1981 - 1/20/1989 Collection: White House Photographic Collection, 1/20/1981 - 1/20/1989 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmadou_Ahidjo_visits_the_White_House_on_July_26,_1982.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmadou Ahidjo was born on 24 August 1924 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garoua">Garoua</a>, a river port on the Benue in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, then a French mandate territory. His father was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> village chief and his mother a Fulani of slave descent. She raised him as a Muslim and sent him to a Quranic school. He later studied at the Ecole Primaire Supérieur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaounde">Yaoundé</a> for a career in the civil service and in 1942 joined the postal service as a radio operator. His postings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douala">Douala</a>, Ngaoundéré, Bertoua and Mokolo made him, according to his official biographer, the first civil servant from the north to work in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ahidjo entered territorial politics in 1946 and became prime minister in February 1958, aged thirty-four. He negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from June to October 1958, and the National Assembly set 1 January 1960 for independence. He faced opposition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-the-peoples-of-cameroon">Union des Populations du Cameroun</a> (UPC), a pro-communist party that rejected French influence; he defeated its rebellion by 1970 with French military help, using emergency powers.</p><p>After the plebiscite in the British Cameroons, the southern part voted to join the rest of Cameroon. Ahidjo and Premier John Foncha drafted a federal constitution at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foumban">Foumban</a>, and on 1 October 1961 the Federal Republic of Cameroon was formed with Ahidjo as president and Foncha as vice president. In 1962 a decree barred criticism of the government, and opposition leaders who opposed a single party were arrested and imprisoned. The Cameroon National Union became the sole party on 1 September 1966. On 20 May 1972 a referendum approved ending the federation, and the United Republic of Cameroon replaced it on 2 June 1972. Many Anglophones were displeased.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ahidjo resigned, ostensibly for health reasons, on 4 November 1982, and Prime Minister Paul Biya succeeded him two days later. He remained head of the party, but a feud developed, and in July 1983 he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In February 1984 he was sentenced to death in absentia for a plot of June 1983, a sentence Biya commuted to life imprisonment; Ahidjo denied involvement.</p><p>He divided his last years between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and died of a heart attack in Dakar on 30 November 1989. A law of December 1991 officially rehabilitated him. An agreement to return his remains was reached in June 2009, but he remained buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>. Oil revenue began to flow in 1977, and the system of patronage he built, in which jobs, licences and contracts came through the president in return for loyalty, is described by the sources as central to his rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmadou Ahidjo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French Cameroon, a French mandate and then a UN trust territory, became independent on 1 January 1960 as the Republic of Cameroon under <b>Ahmadou Ahidjo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cameroon">Cameroon</a></li><li><blockquote>France handed power to <b>Ahmadou Ahidjo</b>, and Cameroon became independent on 1 January 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cameroon">French Cameroon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garoua">Garoua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. Died in Dakar, in what is now <a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.3,13.4&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Garoua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ahmadou Ahidjo"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6121 4208h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6121 4208h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.3,13.4&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
